WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:11.650
<v Dal Lamagna>I'm Dal LaMagna. Ira Glass will be here in a minute. Just bear with me. I was heading to the Middle East, and there were friends of mine who had been there before and they gave me some tips.
<v Dal Lamagna>我是 Dal LaMagna。Ira Glass 一会儿就到。请耐心等我一下。我正要前往中东，以前去过那里的朋友给了我一些建议。

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:12.780
<v Woman 1>Salam aleikum.
<v Woman 1>愿主平安（Salam aleikum）。

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.830
<v Dal Lamagna>Salam aleikum.
<v Dal Lamagna>愿主平安。

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:13.990
<v Man1>That's it.
<v Man1>就是这样。

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.080
<v Woman 1>Alaikum salam.
<v Woman 1>愿主也平安（Alaikum salam）。

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.200
<v Man 1>Alaikum salam.
<v Man 1>愿主也平安。

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.220
<v Dal Lamagna>Aleikum salam. So do I say salam aleikum first?
<v Dal Lamagna>愿主也平安。那我应该先说“愿主平安”吗？

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:21.400
<v Man 1>Depends.
<v Man 1>看情况。

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.250
<v Woman 1>It's a mutual exchange. You'll probably both say it at the same time.
<v Woman 1>这是一种相互交流。你们俩很可能会同时说出来。

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:24.960
<v Dal Lamagna>Salam aleikum.
<v Dal Lamagna>愿主平安。

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:25.250
<v Woman 1>Aleikum salam.
<v Woman 1>愿主也平安。

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:26.320
<v Dal Lamagna>Aleikum salam.
<v Dal Lamagna>愿主也平安。

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.040
<v Woman 1>And say that everywhere. That's going to be very good for you.
<v Woman 1>到处都这么说。这对你会很有好处。

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.760
<v Dal Lamagna>The day before I went, I stopped by Tony, who is a friend of mine and my tailor, to get some clothes.
<v Dal Lamagna>出发前一天，我顺路去找了我的裁缝朋友 Tony，拿些衣服。

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.010
<v Tony>You're sure you're not going to wear a tuxedo there? I thought for sure you'd have to wear the tuxedo.
<v Tony>你确定不去那里穿燕尾服吗？我敢肯定你得穿燕尾服。

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:45.360
<v Dal Lamgana>I'm not going to any parties. I'm meeting with the resistance.
<v Dal Lamgana>我又不参加派对。我要去会见抵抗组织。

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:45.670
<v Tony>Oh.
<v Tony>哦。

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:50.360
<v Dal Lamagna>I'm having meetings with sheikhs. You know?
<v Dal Lamagna>我要去会见谢赫（部落首领）。你懂的？

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:51.750
<v Tony>The sheikhs know good clothes.
<v Tony>谢赫们懂好衣服。

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.970
<v Dal Lamagna>No, they do. They're all dressed in their--
<v Dal Lamagna>不，他们确实懂。他们都穿着他们的——

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.330
<v Tony>They know nice clothes.
<v Tony>他们懂好衣服。

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:07.680
<v Dal Lamagna>I had dinner at my sister's house the night before I left with her and her husband. I was going to Iraq hoping to do some good there. And they weren't very keen about it at all.
<v Dal Lamagna>出发前一晚，我在姐姐家和她及她丈夫共进晚餐。我当时要去伊拉克，希望能在那儿做点好事。但他们一点也不支持。

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.560
<v Sister>Well at least you're trying to do something. I think you're crazy, but at least you're trying to do something.
<v Sister>好吧，至少你在尝试做些什么。我觉得你疯了，但至少你在尝试做些什么。

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.640
<v Brother-in-law>Don Quixote.
<v Brother-in-law>唐吉诃德。

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.240
<v Dal Lamagna>See the attitude I get from people about what I'm doing? OK, here's Ira Glass.
<v Dal Lamagna>看看人们对我的所作所为是什么态度？好了，Ira Glass 来了。

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:42.600
<v Ira Glass>Thanks Dal LaMagna. As you can hear, Dal is somebody who is capable of a lot of things, opening a radio show for instance. And the reason that Dal was going to Iraq was to end the violence there. Literally, to broker a ceasefire between the Sunni resistance and the coalition forces. Basically, he wanted to get Iraqis to stop shooting at Americans.
<v Ira Glass>谢谢 Dal LaMagna。正如你所听到的，Dal 是一个能力很强的人，比如能开办一个广播节目。Dal 去伊拉克的原因是为了结束那里的暴力冲突。从字面上讲，就是为了促成逊尼派抵抗组织和联军之间的停火。基本上，他想让伊拉克人停止向美国人开火。

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:57.330
<v Ira Glass>He doesn't speak Arabic. He's got no policy making experience. He's not a diplomat, or a CIA agent, or an ex-Marine. He's not Bono. He's just a guy, which brings us to today's program. Dal, give them the ID.
<v Ira Glass>他不会说阿拉伯语。他没有政策制定经验。他不是外交官，不是中情局特工，也不是退役海军陆战队员。他不是 Bono。他只是个普通人，这就要引出今天的节目了。Dal，做个自我介绍。

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:06.050
<v Dal Lamagna>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. Want me to do it again?
<v Dal Lamagna>这里是芝加哥 WBEZ，这是《美国生活》，由公共广播国际公司分发。要我重来一遍吗？

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:25.110
<v Ira Glass>No, that was perfect. Today on our show, Man vs. History, we have two stories in two different acts of two different men, regular guys, taking history into their own hands. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>不，那很完美。今天在我们的节目“人与历史”中，我们将讲述两个不同篇章里的两个不同男人的故事，他们都是普通人，将历史掌握在自己手中。请继续收听。

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:48.100
<v Ira Glass>Act One. Well we heard about Dal LaMagna actually by reading about him in the newspaper. There was this article in the New York Times about a Sunni politician from Iraq who was in Washington, DC lobbying on Capitol Hill. And this article mentioned a tweezer magnate, the maker of the Tweezerman tweezer, which at that time I had never heard of, but since then, all the women in my life have informed me is the greatest thing ever.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。我们是通过报纸读到 Dal LaMagna 的。当时《纽约时报》有一篇关于一位伊拉克逊尼派政治家在华盛顿特区国会山游说的文章。文章提到了一个镊子大亨，Tweezerman 镊子的制造商，当时我从未听说过，但从那以后，我生命中所有的女性都告诉我，那是史上最好的东西。

00:02:48.100 --> 00:03:10.280
<v Ira Glass>And this Tweezerman guy, Dal LaMagna, was doing all kinds of things with his millions to advance peace in the Middle East, including setting up meetings between this Sunni politician and members of Congress. But at the time, he was planning what was going to be his biggest project of all, the thing you heard him talk about at the beginning of the show. This mission to get the Sunni resistance to sit down with coalition forces and try to get to terms for a ceasefire.
<v Ira Glass>而这个 Tweezerman 先生，Dal LaMagna，正在利用他的数百万美元做各种事情来推动中东和平，包括安排这位逊尼派政治家与国会议员会面。但当时，他正在计划他最大的项目，也就是你在节目开头听到的那个。他的使命是让逊尼派抵抗组织与联军坐下来，试图达成停火条款。

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:28.360
<v Ira Glass>We heard about that and we wondered, how this is going to go? And so we called him up. And we asked him if he would be willing to take a little recorder with him. He came back with over 25 hours of sound. And This American Life producer Sarah Koenig sifted through all that sound and then had Dal come into the studio to talk about it. Here's Sarah.
<v Ira Glass>我们听说了这件事，心想，这会进展得如何？于是我们给他打了电话。我们问他是否愿意带个小录音机去。他带回了超过 25 小时的音频。《美国生活》制作人 Sarah Koenig 梳理了所有这些音频，然后让 Dal 来到演播室谈论这件事。下面是 Sarah 的报道。

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:51.020
<v Sarah Koenig>When Dal LaMagna gets an idea, he charges at it with everything he's got. He has bankrolled movies. He ran for Congress in 1996 and spent $1 million of his own money on his campaign, literally all of his money at the time, and lost. And then four years later, he ran again. He's the kind of guy who would run for president even though he has no hope of actually becoming president. In fact, right now he is running for president.
<v Sarah Koenig>当 Dal LaMagna 有了个想法，他会全力以赴地去执行。他曾资助过电影。他在 1996 年竞选国会议员，在竞选中花了 100 万美元的个人积蓄，实际上当时他所有的钱都花了，结果落选了。四年后，他又参选了。他就是那种即使毫无希望也会去竞选总统的人。事实上，他现在正在竞选总统。

00:03:51.020 --> 00:04:07.720
<v Sarah Koenig>And so when he got frustrated lobbying Washington about the war, he figured, why try to convince congressmen to convince the president to set a new course with the Iraqis? Cut out the middleman. And so, back in June, he boarded a Royal Jordanian Airlines flight, fell sleep and woke up in Amman, Jordan where his ceasefire project would begin.
<v Sarah Koenig>所以当他在华盛顿游说战争问题感到沮丧时，他想，为什么要费力去说服国会议员去说服总统与伊拉克人开辟新路线呢？直接跳过中间人。于是，回到六月，他登上了皇家约旦航空公司的航班，睡着了，醒来时已经在约旦安曼，他的停火项目将从那里开始。

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.680
<v Guard>Address here?
<v Guard>这边的地址？

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:25.020
<v Dal Lamagna>My address here? I'm staying with Mohammed al-Dynee. al-Dynee. He's a member of the Iraq Parliament. We're staying here.
<v Dal Lamagna>我在这儿的地址吗？我住在 Mohammed al-Dynee 那儿。al-Dynee。他是伊拉克议会的成员。我们住这儿。

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:56.370
<v Sarah Koenig>Dal met Mohammed al-Dynee, the Sunni politician we read about in that New York Times article when Mohammed came to Washington, DC. He's one of the youngest members of Iraq's parliament, and he stayed at Dal's house for three weeks while Dal took him around Congress. And now Mohammed had invited Dal to come stay at his place in Amman. Amman is where lots of Iraqi political players are living now that it's so dangerous in Iraq. So Amman is where Dal and Mohammed will begin to map out a peace agreement for one of the largest regional conflicts in the world. Their strategy? Meetings, lots of meetings.
<v Sarah Koenig>Dal 在那篇《纽约时报》文章中读到的那位逊尼派政治家 Mohammed al-Dynee 到华盛顿特区时，Dal 见了他。他是伊拉克议会最年轻的成员之一，他在 Dal 家住了三周，Dal 带着他跑遍了国会。现在 Mohammed 邀请 Dal 去他在安曼的住处。由于伊拉克局势险恶，许多伊拉克政治人物现在都住在安曼。所以安曼就是 Dal 和 Mohammed 开始为世界上最大地区冲突之一起草和平协议的地方。他们的策略？开会，大量的会议。

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:02.210
<v Mohammed Al-dynee>Tomorrow morning, we're meeting Sheikh Harith al-Dari.
<v Mohammed Al-dynee>明天早上，我们要见 Harith al-Dari 谢赫。

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.000
<v Dal Lamagna>Oh, really.
<v Dal Lamagna>哦，真的吗。

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:23.750
<v Sarah Koenig>Dal's first meeting is with Sheikh Harith al-Dari, a Sunni cleric so important, the story goes, that President Bush has tried to meet with him and the sheikh has declined. And if you're asking why he turned down the president of the United States but said yes to Tweezerman, here's why. Dal had unwittingly become a big deal in Mohammed's political circle because of Mohammed's trip to DC.
<v Sarah Koenig>Dal 的第一次会面是与 Harith al-Dari 谢赫，一位非常重要的逊尼派教士，据说布什总统曾试图见他，但被他拒绝了。如果你问为什么他拒绝了美国总统却答应了 Tweezerman，原因如下。因为 Mohammed 的 DC 之行，Dal 在不知不觉中成了 Mohammed 政治圈里的重要人物。

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:37.060
<v Sarah Koenig>Dal had thought the trip was kind of a bust. Even the New York Times story wasn't exactly positive. It suggested that Mohammed might be a liar, or worse a criminal, which Dal thought was ridiculous, but still. And they didn't meet nearly as many congressmen as they wanted.
<v Sarah Koenig>Dal 本以为那次旅行是个失败。连《纽约时报》的文章也不完全是正面的。它暗示 Mohammed 可能是一个骗子，甚至更糟是一个罪犯，Dal 认为这很荒谬，但事实确实如此。而且他们见到的国会议员远没有他们预想的那么多。

00:05:37.060 --> 00:06:07.530
<v Dal Lamagna>I was very disappointed, but from Mohammed's point of view, and from everybody who he represented this point of view back there, he was the first member of parliament from the national side to get any voice. From their point of view, the story in the New York Times was terrific. The fact that it was a full page story with a picture and the fact that I was the one who facilitated it, raised my status dramatically amongst all these nationalist forces.
<v Dal Lamagna>我非常失望，但从 Mohammed 的角度来看，以及他所代表的所有人的观点来看，他是来自民族方第一个发出声音的议员。在他们看来，《纽约时报》的报道很棒。它是全版报道且配有照片，而我正是促成此事的人，这使我在所有这些民族主义力量中的地位显著提高。

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:37.460
<v Sarah Koenig>This first meeting with Sheikh Harith al-Dari is arguably the most important meeting he'll have his whole trip. Al-Dari is considered hugely influential in Sunni resistance groups, some of the same groups the American government calls insurgents. Their plan is that once Dal has gotten in with Sheikh al-Dari, they can then go see the US commanders in Baghdad. Dal can announce that he's met with the sheikh, the same one they've been trying to talk to but can't. And after that, Dal will become the liaison between the Sunni resistance and the US forces. And they can start to talk ceasefire.
<v Sarah Koenig>与 Harith al-Dari 谢赫的第一次会面可以说是他整趟行程中最重要的一次。Al-Dari 被认为在逊尼派抵抗组织中有巨大的影响力，也就是美国政府所说的叛乱组织。他们的计划是，一旦 Dal 与 al-Dari 谢赫建立了联系，他们就可以去见巴格达的美军指挥官。Dal 可以宣布他见过谢赫了，就是他们一直想谈却谈不到的那位。此后，Dal 将成为逊尼派抵抗组织和美军之间的联络人。他们就可以开始商谈停火了。

00:06:37.460 --> 00:07:08.480
<v Dal Lamagna>So I just left my meeting with Sheikh Harith al-Dari. I wasn't allowed to record it. And so I frantically wrote notes. I've got 35 pages. And the sheikh has a very, very gentle face. He's a very calm man. And we go? OK.
<v Dal Lamagna>所以我刚结束与 Harith al-Dari 谢赫的会面。我不被允许录音。所以我拼命记笔记。我有 35 页。谢赫有一张非常非常温和的脸。他是一个非常冷静的人。我们走吧？好的。

00:07:08.480 --> 00:08:17.210
<v Dal Lamagna>It's two o'clock in the morning. I'm still up. Everybody else is in bed. Yesterday I went to see the sheikh. I guess I would have to admit I was a little nervous. Well this meeting went on for three hours. I was starting to get hot. I was in my suit. And I was looking for my opportunity to ask him the question that I wanted to ask him. Thinking that he actually had the power to call a ceasefire against American soldiers, I said to him, "Look, here's the situation. You need to do something unexpected. If you can do something unexpected, you could break through this stalemate. And an unexpected thing to do would be for the resistance to have a ceasefire, to stop shooting at the American soldiers and to concentrate on Al-Qaeda."
<v Dal Lamagna>现在是凌晨两点。我还没睡。其他人都睡了。昨天我去见了谢赫。我想我得承认我有点紧张。这次会面持续了三个小时。我开始觉得热了。我穿着西装。我在寻找机会问他我想问的问题。我以为他真的有权力呼吁针对美军停火，我对他说：“看，情况是这样的。你需要做一些出人意料的事情。如果你能做些出人意料的事，你就能打破僵局。而一件出人意料的事就是抵抗组织实现停火，停止向美军射击，并将重点转向基地组织。”

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:55.060
<v Dal Lamagna>He looked at me and said, "You know, I can't tell the Iraqis what to do. The Iraqis do what they do and what they want to do. They are against the occupation. And for me, the only way I can influence them is if the Americans announced that they were going to withdraw." Of course as of now, no Iraqi believes America will ever withdraw.
<v Dal Lamagna>他看着我说：“你知道，我不能告诉伊拉克人该做什么。伊拉克人做他们想做的事。他们反对占领。对我来说，只有当美国宣布撤军时，我才能影响他们。”当然，截至目前，没有伊拉克人相信美国会撤军。

00:08:55.060 --> 00:09:08.600
<v Dal Lamagna>And when I went outside, my first though was how I was a little disappointed.
<v Dal Lamagna>当我走出来时，我第一个想法是我有多么失望。

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:31.050
<v Sarah Koenig>But this meeting did two things for Dal. First, the simple fact that he had met with the sheikh, like an audience with royalty, made him someone who everyone wanted to meet with. And second, now at least he knew the starting point for negotiations. The sheikh wouldn't lean on the Sunnis unless the Americans announced a withdrawal. Since America wasn't about to announce a withdrawal, somewhere in there, he'd have to find some wiggle room.
<v Sarah Koenig>但这次会面为 Dal 做了两件事。首先，他见过谢赫这一简单事实，就像觐见皇室成员一样，使他成为了每个人都想见的人。其次，现在他至少知道了谈判的起点。除非美国宣布撤军，否则谢赫不会向逊尼派施压。由于美国不可能宣布撤军，他必须在其中找到一些回旋余地。

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:56.350
<v Sarah Koenig>But this is just day one, a Tuesday. By the following Tuesday, Dal had sat through 15 meetings. There was a school teacher who survived an assassination attempt. Iraq's former minister of oil, a guy who was injured in a suicide bombing. Iraq's ambassador to Jordan, people from Diyala, Anbar, Baghdad Amarah Kirkuk. There was another very important sheikh, this time a shia, who came all the way from Syria just to see Dal.
<v Sarah Koenig>但那只是第一天，周二。到了下个周二，Dal 已经参加了 15 场会议。有一位在暗杀企图中幸存的学校老师。伊拉克前石油部长，一个在自杀式炸弹袭击中受伤的人。伊拉克驻约旦大使，来自迪亚拉、安巴尔、巴格达、阿马拉、基尔库克的人。还有另一位非常重要的谢赫，这次是一位什叶派，特意从叙利亚赶来见 Dal。

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:58.160
<v Syrian Sheikh>[SPEAKING FOREIGN LANGUAGE]
<v Syrian Sheikh>[说着外语]

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:04.130
<v Translator>The fact that if the American troops stay in Iraq, it will definitely lead to a civil war.
<v Translator>如果美军留在伊拉克，那肯定会导致内战的事实。

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:05.990
<v Sarah Koenig>A chief of one of the largest Kurdish tribes.
<v Sarah Koenig>最大的库尔德部落之一的酋长。

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:09.850
<v Kurdish Chief>[SPEAKING FOREIGN LANGUAGE].
<v Kurdish Chief>[说着外语]。

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:13.880
<v Translator>He is saying, with respect, Bush himself has said that he made a mistake.
<v Translator>他说，恕我直言，布什自己也说过他犯了一个错误。

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:18.230
<v Kurdish Chief>[SPEAKING FOREIGN LANGUAGE].
<v Kurdish Chief>[说着外语]。

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:20.690
<v Translator>Condoleezza Rice says we made thousands of mistakes.
<v Translator>康多莉扎·赖斯说我们犯了几千个错误。

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:22.300
<v Sarah Koenig>A group of seven argumentative parliamentarians.
<v Sarah Koenig>一群好辩的议会议员。

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:31.300
<v Parliamentarians>[INTERPOSING VOICES].
<v Parliamentarians>[交错的杂音]。

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:41.340
<v Sarah Koenig>Were you ever thinking, I'm not the guy they think I am? I mean did you ever just worry, they're thinking I'm more influential than I am?
<v Sarah Koenig>你有没有想过，“我不是他们以为的那个人”？我的意思是，你有没有担心过，他们以为你比实际更有影响力？

00:10:41.340 --> 00:11:12.800
<v Dal Lamagna>No, never occurred to me. I don't think that way. I'm an entrepreneur. My entire life, that's what I am, an entrepreneur. And what is an entrepreneur? An entrepreneur is somebody who makes things happen. And I'm making things happen. And I'm not thinking about, oh well, I'm not an important enough person to do this. I'm just making the things happen. And certainly if it was Bill Clinton doing it or Jimmy Carter doing it or anybody else, yeah, it would be big news. And there would be conversations about it. But they're not doing it. So I'm doing it.
<v Dal Lamagna>不，从未想过。我不那样思考。我是个企业家。我的一生，这就是我的本质，一个企业家。什么是企业家？企业家就是能让事情发生的人。我正在让事情发生。我没有在想，哦，我不够重要，做不了这个。我只是在让事情发生。当然，如果是比尔·克林顿或吉米·卡特或其他任何人做这件事，是的，那会是重大新闻。也会有相关的讨论。但他们没做。所以我做了。

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:32.630
<v Sarah Koenig>When you think about how many experts there are out there in this stuff and how many people have been working their whole careers on solving the problems in the Middle East, the fact that you would even fantasize about that sounds a little grandiose and crazy.
<v Sarah Koenig>当你考虑到这个领域有多少专家，有多少人为了解决中东问题奋斗了整个职业生涯，而你居然幻想能做到，这听起来有点宏大且疯狂。

00:11:32.630 --> 00:12:11.500
<v Dal Lamagna>I don't think it's crazy. I'm not a crazy person. I'm a person who, when I founded Tweezerman 25 years ago and I was walking around with this tweezer. And people were saying to me-- I was going to build a company around it-- and they were saying, you should sell it to Revlon. Or you should do this, you should do that. How are you ever going to it out? And there are always naysayers, always people who can't imagine how anybody can accomplish any of the great things that we aspire to. And I did it. And the thought that one could do great things is a reasonable thought. It's not a crazy thought.
<v Dal Lamagna>我不觉得疯狂。我不是个疯子。我是一个人在 25 年前创立 Tweezerman 时，带着这把镊子走来走去。人们对我说——我打算围绕它建立一家公司——他们对我说，你应该把它卖给露华浓（Revlon）。或者你应该这样做，那样做。你怎么可能成功？总会有怀疑者，总会有人无法想象别人如何能实现我们所渴望的任何伟大目标。但我做到了。能做成大事的想法是一个合理的想法。这不是个疯狂的想法。

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:35.670
<v Sarah Koenig>The Iraqis he's talking to, mostly Sunnis, call themselves nationalists, which means they want a secular government that's fair to everyone, Sunni, Shia and Kurd. And so all of them have plenty of complaints about the current Shia led government of Nouri al-Maliki, which has reported ties to Shia militias and death squads. They also hate Iran. But Dal is more interested in solutions than complaints. And he tries to push the conversations toward practical ideas.
<v Sarah Koenig>他交谈的那些伊拉克人，大多是逊尼派，自称民族主义者，意思是他们想要一个对所有人都公平的世俗政府，无论是逊尼派、什叶派还是库尔德人。所以他们对当前努里·马利基领导的什叶派政府有很多抱怨，该政府据传与什叶派民兵和死亡小队有联系。他们也痛恨伊朗。但 Dal 对解决问题比抱怨更感兴趣。他试图将对话引向实用的想法。

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:37.930
<v Dal Lamagna>And the solution then is?
<v Dal Lamagna>那么解决方案是？

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:39.100
<v Parliament Member>[SPEAKING FOREIGN LANGUAGE]
<v Parliament Member>[说着外语]

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:42.630
<v Translator>The solution is to burn Iran from east to west.
<v Translator>解决方案是从东到西烧毁伊朗。

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:55.100
<v Sarah Koenig>Burn Iran from east to west. That's a member of parliament talking by the way. And all this time, Dal is trying to make sense of what he's hearing. Remember, his specialty is selling personal grooming products so even the basics are new to him.
<v Sarah Koenig>从东到西烧毁伊朗。顺便说一下，那是一位议员说的话。而此时此刻，Dal 正试图理解他所听到的东西。记住，他的专长是销售个人护理产品，所以即使是基础知识对他来说也是新的。

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:57.330
<v Dal Lamagna>I thought Al-Qaeda came from Saudi Arabia.
<v Dal Lamagna>我以为基地组织来自沙特阿拉伯。

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:59.230
<v Woman 2>No, they didn't come.
<v Woman 2>不，他们不是来自那儿。

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:00.860
<v Dal Lamagna>Any come from Saudi Arabia?
<v Dal Lamagna>有来自沙特阿拉伯的吗？

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:05.110
<v Woman 2>Jihad is a fight, fighting for your rights.
<v Woman 2>圣战是一种斗争，为了你的权利而奋斗。

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.300
<v Dal Lamagna>And what is mujahideen?
<v Dal Lamagna>那什么是“圣战者”（mujahideen）？

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:10.260
<v Woman 2>Mujahideen means fighters.
<v Woman 2>圣战者意味着战士。

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:16.900
<v Dal Lamagna>And I'm confused. Talabani is the president of Iraq. He has a brother?
<v Dal Lamagna>我糊涂了。塔拉巴尼是伊拉克总统。他有个兄弟？

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:35.250
<v Sarah Koenig>In the 25 hours of Dal's recordings, of course there are moments like this. But there's another side too. We all read the same newspaper stories. We all sit at home and feel horrible about the war. Dal actually did something about it. He found his way to Iraqi politicians. He got on a plane. He's trying.
<v Sarah Koenig>在 Dal 录下的 25 小时里，当然有这样的时刻。但也有另一面。我们都读过同样的报纸报道。我们都坐在家里对战争感到恐惧。Dal 真的为此采取了行动。他找到了通往伊拉克政客的门路。他上了飞机。他在努力。

00:13:35.250 --> 00:14:19.420
<v Dal Lamagna>I'm sitting at the pool at the Marriott hotel at the Dead Sea in Amman, Jordan. So I'm here sitting thinking about this whole idea of what am I doing. Why am I here? What can one American with absolutely no political access-- well, I've learned something from my mother. And that is if your enemy knows you, and you know him, really well, and the better you know each  other, the harder it is to maintain a fight.
<v Dal Lamagna>我坐在约旦安曼死海万豪酒店的泳池边。我坐在这里思考关于我正在做的这件事的整个想法。我为什么在这里？一个完全没有政治背景的美国人能做什么——好吧，我从我母亲那里学到了一些东西。那就是如果你的敌人了解你，你也了解他，非常了解，你们越了解对方，就越难维持一场战斗。

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:40.580
<v Dal Lamagna>My mother used to tie my sister and myself together when we would have our flights until we made up. And it always worked. After being tied together for however long it was, next thing you know we were laughing about it, and we weren't fighting anymore.
<v Dal Lamagna>我母亲以前在我们打架时会把我姐姐和我绑在一起，直到我们和好。这总是奏效。被绑在一起无论多久后，接下来的事情你就会发现我们正对此大笑，不再争斗了。

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:59.230
<v Dal Lamagna>Maybe we get the resistance together with the generals for a weekend at one of these resorts. And have them hang out, and eat dinner together, and eat lunch together and go swimming together.
<v Dal Lamagna>也许我们可以让抵抗组织和将军们在这些度假胜地之一度过周末。让他们聚在一起，一起吃晚饭，一起吃午饭，一起游泳。

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:50.880
<v Dal Lamagna>It's three o'clock Amman time. One of my assistants back in America sent me a link to a Seattle Times posting about the sheikh that I visited the first day I was here. And whoa, just a string of hate comments, people calling me a moron and a traitor. And who the hell do I think I am? Not one positive comment. I mean here I am trying to talk a sheikh into a ceasefire against our soldiers, and they're calling me a traitor.
<v Dal Lamagna>安曼时间三点。我在美国的助手之一发给我一个《西雅图时报》的链接，是关于我到这儿第一天访问的那位谢赫的。哇，简直是一连串的仇恨评论，人们叫我白痴和叛徒。我以为我是谁啊？没有一条正面评论。我是说我在这里试图说服一位谢赫实现针对我们士兵的停火，他们却叫我叛徒。

00:15:50.880 --> 00:16:06.710
<v Sarah Koenig>After a week in Jordan, Mohammed gets a call that Dal's visa to Iraq is ready. As always on this trip, he's getting the kind of treatment a high-level diplomat would get. The Iraqi ambassador to Jordan has personally expedited his paperwork. So now they're ready to go to Baghdad. And they get another important message.
<v Sarah Koenig>在约旦待了一周后，Mohammed 接到通知，说 Dal 去伊拉克的签证准备好了。和这次旅行中的所有事情一样，他得到的待遇是高级外交官才能得到的。伊拉克驻约旦大使亲自加急办理了他的手续。现在他们准备去巴格达了。他们又收到了一个重要信息。

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:29.730
<v Dal Lamagna>The big news is that we actually got a meeting with General Petraeus, which is something we've been trying to do for the last four months, at 11:20 tomorrow in Baghdad at the embassy. We're leaving tomorrow morning on a seven o'clock flight. And so I need to bring a suit, right?
<v Dal Lamagna>重大新闻是，我们真的得到了与彼得雷乌斯将军会面的机会，这是我们过去四个月一直试图做的事情，明天 11 点 20 分在巴格达的使馆。我们明天早上搭乘七点的航班离开。所以我需要带一套西装，对吧？

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:43.350
<v Dal Lamagna>OK, we're here an hour and a half before the plane leaves. So I think it was my tweezers that stopped us at the gate.
<v Dal Lamagna>好了，我们在飞机起飞前一个半小时到了这儿。所以我认为是因为我的镊子在登机口拦住了我们。

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:58.630
<v Dal Lamagna>Yeah well, I'm looking down today at Baghdad. It looks like any other place. Well, there aren't too many trees down there. Looks relatively calm.
<v Dal Lamagna>是啊，我今天俯瞰巴格达。它看起来和其他地方没什么两样。嗯，下面的树不是很多。看起来相对平静。

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:15.670
<v Sarah Koenig>Dal puts on body armor and they head to the Green Zone, where they're scheduled to meet with General David Petraeus, who's in charge of US forces in Iraq. Petraeus's assistant, Mary Kohler, has set it up. And Dal, Mohammed and [? Hasam, ?] their translator, wait for her outside the American embassy so she can escort them in as VIPs.
<v Sarah Koenig>Dal 穿上防弹衣，他们前往绿区，计划在那里会见负责驻伊美军的戴维·彼得雷乌斯将军。彼得雷乌斯的助理 Mary Kohler 安排了这次会面。Dal、Mohammed 和他们的翻译 Hasam 在美国大使馆外等她，以便她能以贵宾身份护送他们进去。

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:17.640
<v Dal Lamagna>Mary?
<v Dal Lamagna>Mary？

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:26.490
<v Mary Kohler>I'm sorry. The General has just been called away. That's why I'm late. So I'm going to have you meet with Major General Newton.
<v Mary Kohler>对不起。将军刚被叫走了。这就是为什么我迟到了。所以我要让你们去见牛顿少将。

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:30.520
<v Dal Lamagna>Oh God. I can't believe it.
<v Dal Lamagna>天哪。我不敢相信。

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:32.930
<v Mary Kohler>He's really sorry.
<v Mary Kohler>他真的很抱歉。

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:58.430
<v Sarah Koenig>Two hours earlier, the Shiite shrine in Samarra had been blown up for the second time, the most serious incident in months. And American commanders were scrambling. The government would declare a curfew and Baghdad would be shut down for days. But of course Dal knows none of this. Since Petraeus can't make it, Mary has arranged for them to sit down with the top British commanders, General Graeme Lamb and Major General Paul Newton. Lamb is in charge of reconciliation in Iraq.
<v Sarah Koenig>两个小时前，萨迈拉的什叶派圣殿第二次被炸毁，这是数月来最严重的事件。美国指挥官们乱作一团。政府宣布宵禁，巴格达将被封锁数日。但 Dal 当然对此一无所知。由于彼得雷乌斯无法出席，Mary 安排他们与英国最高指挥官格雷姆·兰姆将军和保罗·牛顿少将坐下来谈。兰姆负责伊拉克的和解工作。

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:02.160
<v General Lamb>You can tell this is the British office.
<v General Lamb>你可以看出这是英国办公室。

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:04.690
<v Dal Lamagna>Exactly. Basically--
<v Dal Lamagna>正是。基本上——

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:06.160
<v General Lamb>And so you fit in where?
<v General Lamb>那你属于哪一方？

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:07.370
<v Dal Lamagna>I'm Dal LaMagna.
<v Dal Lamagna>我是 Dal LaMagna。

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:07.870
<v General Lamb>Got it.
<v General Lamb>明白了。

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:10.870
<v Dal Lamagna>I'm a citizen diplomat. I'm not--
<v Dal Lamagna>我是公民外交官。我不是——

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:34.320
<v Sarah Koenig>This is what all the meetings had been leading up to. Dal would help Mohammed present the Sunni resistance position to the coalition forces. Finally, he was bringing the two sides together. But right away, the meeting goes off course. First of all, nobody seems much impressed that Dal has met with Sheikh Harith al-Dari, Dal's supposed pipeline to the bigwigs in the Sunni resistance slash insurgency. Sure, al-Dari is a big deal, but he's sitting in Jordan.
<v Sarah Koenig>这就是所有会议要引出的结果。Dal 将帮助 Mohammed 向联军陈述逊尼派抵抗组织的立场。终于，他把双方拉到了一起。但会议一开始就偏离了轨道。首先，似乎没有人对 Dal 会见过 Harith al-Dari 谢赫印象深刻，而那可是 Dal 所谓的通往逊尼派抵抗组织大佬的管道。当然，al-Dari 是个大人物，但他待在约旦。

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:40.620
<v Sarah Koenig>Then Mohammed starts to argue with Lamb. "The Maliki government is corrupt," he says. "Then change it," Lamb says.
<v Sarah Koenig>接着 Mohammed 开始与兰姆争论。“马利基政府是腐败的，”他说。“那就换掉它，”兰姆说。

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:50.920
<v General Lamb>But the constitution is very clear. If you wish to challenge the government through the constitutional process, that is open to this parliament.
<v General Lamb>但宪法非常明确。如果你希望通过宪法程序挑战政府，那是对议会开放的。

00:18:50.920 --> 00:19:13.830
<v Sarah Koenig>But Mohammed says even showing up to debate the constitution could get them killed by Maliki's people. This goes around and around. Mohammed raises all the complaints that Sunnis have been talking about all week in their meetings with Dal, the open border with Iran, how dangerous it is for Sunni members of parliament, the crooked Shia police. And the generals keep repeating, take it up with the Maliki government. And then Dal steps in.
<v Sarah Koenig>但 Mohammed 说，即使出席宪法辩论也可能让他们被马利基的人杀掉。这就成了循环。Mohammed 提出了逊尼派整周在与 Dal 会面时谈论的所有抱怨，通往伊朗的边境开放，逊尼派议员有多危险，腐败的什叶派警察。而将军们一直重复，去向马利基政府反映。这时 Dal 插话了。

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:29.810
<v Dal Lamagna>It's not useful here in this meeting to argue with the general about the failures and the problems with the Maliki government. It's just not useful because there's nothing they can do.
<v Dal Lamagna>在这次会议上与将军争论马利基政府的失败和问题是没有用的。这没用，因为他们无能为力。

00:19:29.810 --> 00:19:47.730
<v Sarah Koenig>The top commanders in Iraq say they make it a practice to listen to nobodies who might turn out to be somebody. They're smart enough to know that they can't predict where progress might lurk. But they're not in the practice of wasting time. Finally General Newton asks them in his polite, British roundabout way, what exactly are you doing here?
<v Sarah Koenig>伊拉克的高级指挥官们说，他们惯于倾听可能成为重要人物的无名小卒。他们足够聪明，知道自己无法预知进步可能潜伏在哪里。但他们没习惯浪费时间。终于，牛顿将军用那种礼貌、委婉的英国方式问他们，你们到底是来干什么的？

00:19:47.730 --> 00:20:15.980
<v Major General Newton>Do you mind if I ask a question? What General Lamb is painting here is that we are not in a position to do grand deals behind the back of the sovereign, elected government. However, naturally we have channels of communication open to people who can deliver a reduction in bombs. I am unclear exactly what is being proposed. That we should be put in touch with specific groups?
<v Major General Newton>你不介意我问个问题吧？兰姆将军在这里描述的是，我们无权在主权选举产生的政府背后做大交易。然而，我们自然有与那些能带来炸弹袭击减少的人进行交流的渠道。我不太清楚到底提出了什么建议。是说我们应该与特定的组织接触吗？

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:23.420
<v General Lamb>What is being offered in the way of a practical opportunity for dialogue?
<v General Lamb>关于实际对话的机会提供了什么？

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:30.870
<v Sarah Koenig>Here's when it becomes clear that the generals see them as essentially empty-handed. They'd love a ceasefire, but is anyone actually offering one?
<v Sarah Koenig>这时很清楚，将军们认为他们基本上是空手而来。他们乐于见到停火，但有人真的在提供吗？

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:42.770
<v Major General Newton>If there is a firm proposal involving being able to take that dialogue forward with armed groups, then I haven't heard what the specific proposal is.
<v Major General Newton>如果有一个确定的提案，涉及能够与武装组织推进对话，那么我还没听到具体的提案是什么。

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:45.140
<v Dal Lamagna>Right, that's good.
<v Dal Lamagna>对，很好。

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:46.560
<v Sarah Koenig>Mohammed speaks up.
<v Sarah Koenig>Mohammed 发言了。

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:56.830
<v Mohammed Al-dynee>We have eight groups, eight major groups. Those people are ready to start talking, but it will be a confidential talk only with the coalition forces.
<v Mohammed Al-dynee>我们有八个组织，八个主要组织。这些人准备开始谈话，但仅限于与联军进行保密谈话。

00:20:56.830 --> 00:21:09.110
<v Dal Lamagna>That's constructive. So he has eight groups. We'll start a dialogue. And they'll decide how to handle it. But let's end on that note.
<v Dal Lamagna>那有建设性。所以他有八个组织。我们将开始对话。他们会决定如何处理。但我们就此结束吧。

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:18.700
<v Sarah Koenig>The generals don't say anything. The meeting ends. Everyone seems a little testy. But Dal is feeling hopeful, until as they're leaving, Lamb says something to Dal.
<v Sarah Koenig>将军们什么也没说。会议结束了。每个人似乎都有点急躁。但 Dal 感到充满希望，直到他们离开时，兰姆对 Dal 说了些话。

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:20.600
<v General Lamb>For four years I've been having these conversations.
<v General Lamb>四年来我一直进行着这些对话。

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:23.450
<v Dal Lamagna>Oh my God.
<v Dal Lamagna>哦，我的天。

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:54.690
<v Dal Lamagna>Lamb's parting shot to me was when I tried to get his contact information and he wouldn't give it to me was, look I've been doing this for four years. I can't tell you how many meetings I've had with people saying they represent the resistance or this is going to happen and that is going to happen. So he stuck a pin in the balloon that had filled up during that meeting at the very end. Yeah, so I went from one high to the low.
<v Dal Lamagna>当我试图获取他的联系方式而他拒绝给我时，兰姆给我的临别赠言是，看，我已经做了四年了。我无法告诉你我与多少人开了会，说他们代表抵抗组织，或者这要发生那要发生。所以他在会议最后，把我那个充满希望的气球戳破了。是的，所以我从一个高潮跌落到了低谷。

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:56.940
<v Sarah Koenig>Because he said that to you?
<v Sarah Koenig>因为他对你说了那些话？

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:17.130
<v Dal Lamagna>That that was like, oh my God, could this have just been theater? All right. This guy from America who's here. And he got here. He's here with this member of parliament. We have to meet with them. Let's just humor them.
<v Dal Lamagna>那简直就像是，天哪，这难道只是表演吗？好吧。这个来自美国的家伙在这里。他到了这里。他和这位议员在一起。我们必须见他们。只是敷衍一下他们吧。

00:22:17.130 --> 00:23:24.540
<v Dal Lamagna>Well it was rather difficult for me to even get out of bed this morning after my meetings with these two generals yesterday. But there a moment there yesterday when I was in a meeting with General Lamb where I swore to myself that I was in one meeting and Mohammed was in another. And I just got the reality that the people I'm talking to, they're so out of the game, so deluded themselves into what is that can happen. And I don't mean to be disparaging. It's almost like I feel as if I should just pack up my bags and go home and forget the whole thing. And you're just feeling-- you run around and now you find out your assumptions about what can be done are wrong.
<v Dal Lamagna>嗯，在昨天与这两位将军开会后，我今天早上甚至很难起床。但在昨天的会议中，有那么一刻，当我和兰姆将军在一起时，我发誓我当时在一场会议中，而 Mohammed 在另一场。我意识到与我交谈的这些人，他们离游戏太远了，对自己能发生的事情产生了幻觉。我不是要贬低谁。我感觉好像我应该收拾行李回家，把整件事忘掉。你只是感觉——你到处奔波，现在发现你对可以做什么的假设是错误的。

00:23:24.540 --> 00:23:58.300
<v Sarah Koenig>But he doesn't pack up and leave. He wakes up on Saturday and tells the tape recorder, "You don't wait for things to happen. The entrepreneurial way is to make things happen." After just two days, he's back on mission. He decides the generals weren't humoring him. They were giving him an opportunity. He and Mohammed just need to present them a concrete proposal, which they draw up, a three page document that's vague about a lot of things but does mention a timetable for US withdrawal and a ceasefire. Dal sends it to the generals. Weeks pass. He never hears anything back. Dal is pretty disappointed.
<v Sarah Koenig>但他没有收拾行李离开。他周六醒来对录音机说：“你不要等事情发生。企业家的做法是让事情发生。”仅仅两天后，他又回到了任务中。他决定将军们并没有敷衍他。他们是在给他一个机会。他和 Mohammed 只需要向他们提出一个具体提案，他们起草了一份三页的文件，对许多事情含糊其辞，但确实提到了美军撤军的时间表和停火。Dal 把文件发给了将军们。几周过去了。他从未得到任何回音。Dal 非常失望。

00:23:58.300 --> 00:24:02.650
<v Dal Lamagna>We've had our meetings. Thank you very much. And it's over. I mean that's--
<v Dal Lamagna>我们已经开了会。非常感谢。一切都结束了。我是说那是——

00:24:02.650 --> 00:24:04.560
<v Sarah Koenig>So you're not hopeful about--
<v Sarah Koenig>所以你不抱希望了？

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:04.870
<v Dal Lamagna>No.
<v Dal Lamagna>不。

00:24:04.870 --> 00:24:07.310
<v Sarah Koenig>Does that mean you've given up?
<v Sarah Koenig>这意味着你放弃了吗？

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:41.150
<v Dal Lamagna>I've given up on the strategy of helping to broker a deal between the national forces and the coalition forces. I've given up on that. So now I'm on to the other strategy. One thing that I have to say about myself, and I think it's a good trait, is that I don't get trapped by my imagined solutions for things. When I can see it's not working, I move on and I try something else. And eventually I'll find something that does work.
<v Dal Lamagna>我放弃了帮助促成民族力量与联军之间达成协议的策略。我放弃了那个。所以现在我转向了另一种策略。关于我自己我必须说的一点，我认为这是一个好品质，那就是我不会被我自己构思的解决方案所困住。当我看到它行不通时，我就转向并尝试其他东西。最终我会找到行得通的东西。

00:24:41.150 --> 00:25:09.410
<v Sarah Koenig>Here's his latest solution. He's using videotape of the meetings he held in Amman to make commercials. In them, Iraqis say point blank what they want us to do, which is leave Iraq. He's using them as the ads for his presidential campaign. And they're definitely unlike any other candidate advertising you've ever seen. He has gone from citizen diplomat to citizen ad man. He'll fight this war stateside for now.
<v Sarah Koenig>这是他最新的解决方案。他正在利用他在安曼举行的会议的录像制作广告。在广告中，伊拉克人直言不讳地表达了他们想让我们做什么，那就是离开伊拉克。他正在把它们用作竞选总统的广告。它们绝对不同于你见过的任何其他候选人广告。他已经从公民外交官变成了公民广告人。他现在要在国内为这场战争而战。

00:25:09.410 --> 00:25:20.600
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Koenig. Coming up, we switch from America's current war to the good old days of the Cold War. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
