00:46:39.040 --> 00:47:15.560
<v Arthur Phillips>At the steering committee's disorganized meeting on the first of August, Johnny 1950 asked the committee if, considering how little information Source Prep School had provided in the preceding weeks, they thought the time had come to squeeze him, to tell him he'd better start coughing up or the news of his betrayals and some compelling video would be sent to his ambassador. Jarmila argued that this would be a mistake. She just had a feeling. She didn't mention to the committee that she'd rather by a long shot spend evenings with a slavishly devoted lover than have some moron from manipulation pretend to be Igor and run the blackmail while she took a dull holiday on the Black Sea.
<v Arthur Phillips>在 8 月 1 日指导委员会杂乱无章的会议上，Johnny 1950 问委员会，考虑到“预备学校来源”在过去几周提供的消息很少，他们是否认为现在是时候施压了，告诉他最好开始吐露真相，否则他背叛的消息和一些令人信服的录像带将被送到他大使那里。Jarmila 反驳说那将是一个错误。她只是有种感觉。她没告诉委员会，她宁愿花晚上与一个奴隶般忠诚的情人在一起，也不愿让某个负责操纵的白痴假装是 Igor 来进行勒索，而她却在黑海度过一个沉闷的假期。

00:47:15.560 --> 00:47:23.050
<v Arthur Phillips>"Be patient with him," she told the committee. "Prep School will produce. I know he will."
<v Arthur Phillips>“对他耐心点，”她告诉委员会。“预备学校会出结果的。我知道他会。”

00:47:23.050 --> 00:47:41.260
<v Arthur Phillips>Meanwhile, Tyler watched as communism crumbled in Hungary and Poland. And he had the strangest sensation as he read each unimaginable dispatch from these other countries. He prayed the democratic virus would be contained, that the Czechs would hold on. But then, the politburo resigned.
<v Arthur Phillips>与此同时，Tyler 看着共产主义在匈牙利和波兰崩溃。当他读到来自这些国家的每一份不可思议的简报时，他有一种奇怪的感觉。他祈祷民主病毒会被控制住，祈祷捷克人会坚持下去。但随后，政治局辞职了。

00:47:41.260 --> 00:47:46.960
<v Arthur Phillips>[PROTESTING]
<v Arthur Phillips>[抗议]

00:47:46.960 --> 00:48:20.170
<v Arthur Phillips>December 3. They walked, side by side, as 150,000 protested the communists' last-ditch effort to allow only a minority opposition presence in a new government. They did not hold hands. They pretended to smile at what they saw. Neither of them spoke. Tyler the traitor should have been delighted. It's over. She's free. He did his dirty bit for her without hurting anyone or getting caught. And now it's passed. Jarmila the oppressed should have been delighted. Her country was free. Her future was her own.
<v Arthur Phillips>12 月 3 日。他们并肩走着，15 万人抗议共产党在选举新政府时只允许少数反对派存在的最后努力。他们没有牵手。他们假装对所看到的一切微笑。他们都没说话。叛徒 Tyler 本应感到高兴。一切都结束了。她自由了。他为她完成了肮脏的工作，没伤害任何人，也没被抓到。现在一切都过去了。受压迫的 Jarmila 本应感到高兴。她的国家自由了。她的未来属于她自己。

00:48:20.170 --> 00:48:36.690
<v Arthur Phillips>If, in character, he told her now she should come with him to the West, escape Igor and their shameful past, but she said no, and she certainly would, then he would be forced to ask her why not. And she would be forced to admit that she'd been lying to him all along and their time together would end.
<v Arthur Phillips>如果，他现在告诉她应该和他一起去西方，逃离 Igor 和他们可耻的过去，但她说不——她肯定会说不——那么他将被迫问她为什么。而她将被迫承认她一直以来都在骗他，他们在一起的时间也将结束。

00:48:36.690 --> 00:49:07.010
<v Arthur Phillips>Or he could tell her he knew she was a spy for a communist spy service. And now, in the new world unfolding all around them, she would be in danger of arrest and retribution. So she should come to the US, be his pet defector. And they could be together. Of course that would mean admitting that he'd been lying to her since the beginning. That there was no Tyler the traitor. That there was nothing about him that she actually knew. That revelation would also be his first act of actual treachery. It was impossible.
<v Arthur Phillips>或者他可以告诉她他知道她是共产主义间谍机构的间谍。现在，在他们周围展开的新世界里，她将面临被捕和报复的危险。所以她应该去美国，成为他的宠物叛逃者。他们可以待在一起。当然，那将意味着承认他从一开始就在骗她。不存在什么叛徒 Tyler。关于他的一切她实际上都一无所知。那个揭露也将是他第一次真正的背叛行为。这不可能。

00:49:07.010 --> 00:49:23.470
<v Arthur Phillips>She watched him. Something was not right here. According to all logic, he should have been happy to see the changes underway. He should have been offering her a way out with him. This should mean the end of his spying for her. This should mean the end of Igor's control over her. So why was he silent?
<v Arthur Phillips>她看着他。这里有些不对劲。按照所有逻辑，看到正在发生的变化他应该感到高兴。他应该给她提供一条和他一起离开的出路。这意味着他为她做间谍活动的结束。这意味着 Igor 对她控制的结束。那么他为什么沉默？

00:49:23.470 --> 00:49:58.000
<v Arthur Phillips>For that matter, why wasn't he telling her to come away with him to Cape Cod? To a wedding on his parents' lawn? Frolicks with some new Labrador? She saw it clearly. He was a liar. That was why. He felt no slavish love for her. He had done what he had done out of lust and weakness, and then lazy momentum, and fear of being blackmailed and nothing else. He was precisely what the playwrights and the planners on the steering committees had always said he was, a depressive, a weakling, lonely and vulnerable, unprincipled and uninterested. In other words, he was an ordinary man.
<v Arthur Phillips>说到这一点，他为什么不告诉她和他一起去科德角？去他父母草坪上的婚礼？去和一只新的拉布拉多犬嬉戏？她看得很清楚。他是个骗子。就是这样。他一点也不觉得对他奴隶般的爱。他做那些事只是出于欲望和软弱，然后是懒散的惯性，以及对被勒索的恐惧，除此之外别无其他。他正是剧作家和指导委员会策划者们一直所说的那样，一个忧郁的人、懦夫、孤独且脆弱、毫无原则且毫无兴趣。换句话说，他是一个普通人。

00:49:58.000 --> 00:50:10.220
<v Arthur Phillips>She felt foolish. Did this egotist really fear she would want to come away with him? Had she painted herself as needy as all that? How embarrassing now in daylight.
<v Arthur Phillips>她感到愚蠢。这个利己主义者真的害怕她会想和他一起走吗？她把自己塑造得那么需要人吗？现在在日光下真是太尴尬了。

00:50:10.220 --> 00:50:41.190
<v Arthur Phillips>Tyler looked at her. She had no real feeling for him. That was clear. If any part of her had felt something real in this little farce of theirs, she would say so now. True, she hadn't seen the best of him. She was judging from false evidence. But surely, she'd seen something under that stuff. Surely she'd been having fun too. Surely also he had been the very opposite of emotionally unavailable, artificial, perfect, untouchable, impenetrable, affectless.
<v Arthur Phillips>Tyler 看着她。她对他没有任何真实的感觉。那是显而易见的。如果她在这场滑稽戏中感受到任何真实的东西，她现在就会说出来。确实，她没见过他最好的一面。她是在根据虚假的证据进行评判。但肯定，她已经看到了那层东西下的一些东西。肯定她也玩得很开心。肯定他也与情感疏离、虚假、完美、不可触碰、难以捉摸、无动于衷完全相反。

00:50:41.190 --> 00:51:09.230
<v Arthur Phillips>Complete silence, except for the throng of cheering, pot-banging Czechs who washed into them. He moved to their left. She moved to their right. The crowd swelled and they both kept walking, not even looking up to be sure they had lost sight of each other.
<v Arthur Phillips>一片寂静，除了涌向他们的欢呼、敲打锅碗瓢盆的捷克人群。他向左走。她向右走。人群拥挤，他们都继续走着，甚至没抬头确认是否已经失去了对方的踪影。

00:51:09.230 --> 00:51:30.100
<v Arthur Phillips>A month earlier, the IRS had found lumpy, indigestible discrepancies in Michael Boortz's tax returns. An investigation soon revealed that he had 13 separate savings accounts. The subsequent CIA interrogation of a sobbing, farting Boortz revealed much of what Boortz had betrayed over the years, including Operation Brief Encounter.
<v Arthur Phillips>一个月前，美国国税局在 Michael Boortz 的纳税申报单中发现了笨重、难以消化的差异。一项调查很快揭露他有 13 个独立的储蓄账户。随后中情局对一个抽泣、放屁的 Boortz 的审讯揭露了 Boortz 多年来背叛的大部分内容，包括“短暂相遇行动”。

00:51:30.100 --> 00:51:43.970
<v Arthur Phillips>And so, before his arrest, and in exchange for vague promises of leniency at his trial, Boortz delivered a final flurry of reports to his Soviet handlers, including one put together by the operational guidance group at the request of Ed Marshall.
<v Arthur Phillips>因此，在他被捕前，作为交换对审判从轻处理的模糊承诺，Boortz 向他的苏联操纵者发送了最后一份报告，包括一份由 Ed Marshall 要求操作指导小组编制的报告。

00:51:43.970 --> 00:52:00.530
<v Arthur Phillips>Jarmila Herbeck, said his report, had been a CIA agent all along. When Vanalden gave her false information, she had knowingly passed it on. And when she was out of her boss's view, she had delivered Czech secrets to Vanalden. This happened in the countryside on picnics.
<v Arthur Phillips>他的报告称，Jarmila Herbeck 自始至终都是中情局的特工。当 Vanalden 给她虚假信息时，她明知而传了出去。当她不在老板视线范围内时，她向 Vanalden 传递了捷克的秘密。这是在乡间的野餐时发生的。

00:52:00.530 --> 00:52:18.850
<v Arthur Phillips>Well, it was better than nothing, Marshall figured. It would throw the remaining Czechs into chaos. But that was a small comfort when you admitted you had just spent months accomplishing exactly nothing. The poor kid. He called Tyler in, told him he'd been doing a stellar job. Marshall himself was putting Tyler up for an intelligence medal.
<v Arthur Phillips>好吧，总比什么都没有强，Marshall 想。这会使剩下的捷克人陷入混乱。但当你承认你只是花了几个月时间什么也没做到时，那点安慰微不足道。可怜的孩子。他叫来 Tyler，说他一直做得非常出色。Marshall 自己正在为 Tyler 申请情报奖章。

00:52:18.850 --> 00:52:33.940
<v Arthur Phillips>"The downside though, Ty, is a little more complex. It would appear that possibly they knew about you, about us, from a pretty early time. Actually likely."
<v Arthur Phillips>“不过缺点是，Ty，有点复杂。看来可能他们很早就知道你了，关于我们。事实上很可能。”

00:52:33.940 --> 00:53:08.380
<v Arthur Phillips>Tyler knew he should be depressed. His life and work for months had been a travesty. He was a videotaped joke. Yet he felt some growing sensation of happiness. Why had the Czechs kept it going if they knew it was garbage? Because she had wanted to. She must have argued for it, for him, propped up the whole shabby structure with justifications just to see him. Every time she met him had been a victory for the two of them, snatched from a dubious Czech service.
<v Arthur Phillips>Tyler 知道他应该沮丧。他的生活和工作几个月来简直是闹剧。他是一个被录像的笑话。然而，他感到一种增长的幸福感。如果捷克人知道这是垃圾，为什么还要让它继续下去？因为她想要。她肯定为此争论过，为他，支撑起整个破旧的结构，仅仅是为了见他。她每次见到他都是他们两人的胜利，从可疑的捷克情报部门中夺回的胜利。

00:53:08.380 --> 00:53:29.660
<v Arthur Phillips>"Sit down," said Johnny 1950. She had come in to see if there was an office at all today, the day the communist president swore in an opposition government and then resigned. The few people still in the office were visibly worried. Nobody knew if they were going to be the secret service of the new republic or a pile of corpses shot for their work on behalf of the old republic.
<v Arthur Phillips>“坐下，”Johnny 1950 说。她进来看看今天办公室是否还在，就在共产主义总统宣誓就职反对派政府然后辞职的那天。办公室里仅剩的几个人显得非常担心。没人知道他们将成为新共和国的秘密警察，还是因为替旧共和国工作而被枪杀的一堆尸体。

00:53:29.660 --> 00:53:47.750
<v Arthur Phillips>"There is some news," said Johnny, "I told you to sit down." He was such a turd, this little man, all bossy and official now of all times. "News?" she said, unconcerned. "Yes," he said. "About two weeks ago, Source Prep School was arrested and shipped back to the USA."
<v Arthur Phillips>“有一些新闻，”Johnny 说，“我叫你坐下。”他真是个废物，这个小个子男人，现在这个时候竟还摆出一副老板和官方的架子。“新闻？”她问道，无动于衷。“是的，”他说。“大约两周前，‘预备学校来源’被捕并被运回了美国。”

00:53:47.750 --> 00:54:01.230
<v Arthur Phillips>She was confused and then realized Tyler was going to jail now. And she wondered if and how she could save him. A sentimental, girlish thought that offended her even as it occurred to her. She nodded, said nothing, turned back to her desk.
<v Arthur Phillips>她感到困惑，然后意识到 Tyler 现在要坐牢了。她想知道她是否以及如何能救他。一个感伤、幼稚的想法，即使在脑海中闪现时也冒犯了她。她点点头，什么也没说，转回办公桌。

00:54:01.230 --> 00:54:22.550
<v Arthur Phillips>"Wait, don't ask many questions yet," said Johnny. "It gets worse." He examined her beautiful face as he told her that they had been holding a losing hand. Prep School was CIA from the very first pass, not State. And she had never noticed. Her sacrifices had been worthless, Johnny repeated twice more for good measure, trying to soften her up.
<v Arthur Phillips>“等等，先别问那么多问题，”Johnny 说。“情况变得更糟了。”他打量着她美丽的脸，告诉她他们一直拿着一手烂牌。Tyler 自始至终都是中情局的，而不是国务院的。而她从未察觉。她的牺牲毫无价值，Johnny 又重复了两次以示强调，试图让她软化。

00:54:22.550 --> 00:54:34.350
<v Arthur Phillips>"Wait," he said with a mix of pity and vengeance and pride at knowing more than anyone else. "It gets worse." She laughed out loud. "Yes worse," he said.
<v Arthur Phillips>“等等，”他说，混合了怜悯、报复和比别人知道得更多的自豪感。“情况变得更糟了。”她大声笑了出来。“是的，更糟，”他说。

00:54:34.350 --> 00:55:21.720
<v Arthur Phillips>"A report has come in from our old friends, perhaps ex-friends, to the East, saying that you have known since day one that Prep School was CIA. Wait, don't talk. It's better if you don't say a word just yet. This report also says you have been sending things the other direction, working for the Americans the whole time. Don't speak. At first, management was going to have you arrested but we actually don't have anyone who does that sort of thing for us right now. We're in a vacuum. Listen and think. If you did this, no one is sure if that's good or bad. If you did this, no one knows what to do about it. Maybe you're a traitor to the Czech people, but maybe you're a hero of the revolution. Don't, don't speak."
<v Arthur Phillips>“收到了一份来自我们东方的老朋友，也许是前朋友的报告，说你从第一天起就知道‘预备学校’是中情局的。等等，别说话。最好现在别说一个字。这份报告还说你一直在反方向发送东西，一直为美国人工作。别说话。起初，管理层打算逮捕你，但我们现在其实没人做那种事。我们处于真空状态。听着，思考一下。如果你这样做了，没人确定那是好是坏。如果你这样做了，没人知道该怎么办。也许你是捷克人民的叛徒，但也许你是革命的英雄。别，别说话。”

00:55:21.720 --> 00:55:40.090
<v Arthur Phillips>Johnny's tone softened. "Don't worry Jarmila. I can handle this for us. I know how these people think. I grew up among the people you know. You and I, we can help each other."
<v Arthur Phillips>Johnny 的语气缓和了。“别担心 Jarmila。我可以帮我们处理。我知道这些人是怎么想的。我在你认识的人中间长大。你和我，我们可以互相帮助。”

00:55:40.090 --> 00:56:05.070
<v Arthur Phillips>The department of personnel approved Tyler's quick, unsolicited proposal, agreeing that the venture would place him near potential sources, and that the role fit his real personality and official history excellently. And so in May, 1990, four months after he'd been bundled out of Prague, he returned, a vaguely disgraced but un-indicted ex-diplomat running his own business, hawking to the new democracy an expertise they were sure to need.
<v Arthur Phillips>人事部门批准了 Tyler 快速、主动提出的建议，同意该项目将使他接近潜在的消息来源，并且该角色非常适合他的真实性格和官方历史。因此，在 1990 年 5 月，也就是他被匆忙赶出布拉格四个月后，他回来了，一个模糊受辱但未被起诉的前外交官，经营着自己的生意，向新的民主政权兜售他们肯定需要的专业知识。

00:56:05.070 --> 00:56:34.370
<v Arthur Phillips>He sat in his hotel room and watched a documentary on Czech TV. It had been made in April by a once banned filmmaker, and was written and produced by a Johnny McDougal. The Revolution's Hidden Heroes it was called. And it profiled those extraordinary souls who had secretly helped the democratic cause from the inside. She looked good on television. Even better as a brunette.
<v Arthur Phillips>他坐在酒店房间里看捷克电视上的纪录片。那是 4 月由一位曾经被禁的电影制作人制作的，由 Johnny McDougal 编写和制作。“革命的隐形英雄”是它的名字。它描绘了那些从内部秘密帮助民主事业的非凡灵魂。她在电视上看起来不错。作为黑发女郎更好。

00:56:34.370 --> 00:57:00.460
<v Arthur Phillips>The president of Vanalden Communications paid $500 to attend a meet-and-greet in the banquet room of the Palace Hotel. He arrived two hours early, examined the seating arrangement, and discreetly changed it. He returned later to the crowded room alongside representatives of a dozen other political consulting firms. And he drank cocktails and chatted with an assortment of civic forum candidates running in the first free Czech election since 1946.
<v Arthur Phillips>Vanalden 通讯公司的总裁支付了 500 美元参加皇宫酒店宴会厅的见面会。他提前两个小时到达，检查了座位安排，并谨慎地进行了更改。他后来与十几家其他政治咨询公司的代表一起回到了拥挤的房间。他喝着鸡尾酒，与参加自 1946 年以来第一次自由捷克选举的各种公民论坛候选人聊天。

00:57:00.460 --> 00:57:24.410
<v Arthur Phillips>Dinner was served. He took his time going to the seat he had usurped. "You look like someone I once knew," he said to the parliamentary candidate seated to his left. "I have that kind of face," she said, smiling. "I saw you on television," he said. "You sacrificed terribly for the revolution." She laughed. "You can't begin to imagine."
<v Arthur Phillips>晚餐开始了。他花时间走到他篡夺的座位上。“你看上去像我曾经认识的某个人，”他对坐在他左边的议会候选人说。“我有那种脸，”她笑着说。“我在电视上见过你，”他说。“你为革命做出了巨大的牺牲。”她笑了。“你无法想象。”

00:57:24.410 --> 00:57:48.690
<v Arthur Phillips>"You know," he said, "I think I could be of great help to you in your campaign." "I'd have to consult with my fiance," she said, gesturing to the suddenly despondent bald man who, far away down the long table, had just recognized Vanalden. "But I doubt he will protest. Tell me about yourself."
<v Arthur Phillips>“你知道，”他说，“我想我在你的竞选中能帮上大忙。”“我得咨询我的未婚夫，”她说，指着远方长桌旁那位突然感到绝望的秃头男人，他刚刚认出了 Vanalden。“但我怀疑他会抗议。告诉我关于你自己的事。”

00:57:48.690 --> 00:58:08.650
<v Ira Glass>Arthur Phillips, reading an abridged version of his story, "Wenceslas Square," which first appeared in a collection of essays and short stories entitled Wild East , Stories From the Last Frontier. Arthur Phillip's most recent novel is Angelica .
<v Ira Glass>Arthur Phillips，朗读他故事“瓦茨拉夫广场”的删节版，该故事首次出现在题为《狂野东部：来自最后前沿的故事》的论文和短篇小说集中。Arthur Phillips 的最新小说是《当归》。

00:58:08.650 --> 00:58:12.020
